[{:seon.task/name "the-judge-reads-run-tests-results"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: an agent's run carries clojure.test's report format end to end: `seon.agent/run-tests-here` collects each assertion as a report map (`seon.agent/test-report`), and the judge and the turn read them back through `seon.proofs/results`, `seon.proofs/red-reports`, `seon.proofs/failures`, `seon.proofs/test-run-errors` and `seon.proofs/costs`, while `seon.proofs/run-tests!` already answers one result per test ({:test :digest :pass :fail :error :ms}, a throw's :message and :data). Correct: the judge (`seon.reconcile/done-explain`'s input), the turn (`seon.turn/observe`), the task's facts (:seon.task/failures, :seon.task/costs) and the context read run-tests!'s result map, each red test keeping its failing assertions (expected, actual, got); the report vector, `test-report` and the readers that rebuild a status per test from it go. About -30 lines in src. Proofs: the done error, the run's failures and costs, and the context's red block read the same from the result map as today from the reports."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/done-error-run-s-failures-costs-context-s}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-runs-the-binary-through-binary-sh"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-10-23 (shrink session), partly done: seon.config-test/bin-ports-writes-its-keys-under-their-current-names now runs bin/ports through `seon.test-store/binary-sh`, the one call `seon.program/graph-index`'s :binary? counts (:seon.config.source/binary-vars), but seon.config-test/bin-ports-writes-at-once-from-many-callers still runs bin/ports (which runs ./seon ports) through clojure.java.shell/sh, so bin/test's plan does not build target/seon for it, and on a worktree whose binary is another tree's it is red for a reason that is not the change (config refused: unknown keys). Correct: every test that runs the binary or a script that runs it (bin/root, bin/check, bin/ports, bin/mcp, ./seon) does so through binary-sh, and a law over test/'s forms (read as data) is red on a shell call naming one of them another way; seon.check-test/every-test-root-starts-through-start-root! is that law for roots only."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/law-over-test-forms-red-any-shell-call}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agent-reads-its-own-recorded-results"
  :seon.task/plan "Answered by the verdict cache (docs/merging.md#verdicts-are-a-cache): every run, a turn's, the judge's or a checkout's, writes and reads one cache by digest, so a red the agent's turn just cached is the judge's at the same digest, reused with its failures, and nothing waits on a merge of main. Left: its proof, a judge after a red turn that forks nothing."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.agent-test/a-judge-reuses-the-red-its-turn-recorded}}
 {:seon.task/name "root-shows-the-results"
  :seon.task/plan "Root's render shows main (seon.render.proc). The verdicts are a cache by digest (docs/merging.md#verdicts-are-a-cache), so a render over the one printer (docs/render.md) shows, where root's page shows problems, main's verdicts at its reach digests (seon.fx/stored-results over main's plan): the flaky digests, the reds, and the costliest functions per call over them (seon.proofs/per-call over seon.fx/details). A render shows what is cached, never computes it again (docs/render.md#a-render-does-no-work-of-its-own)."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/root-s-page-lists-main-s-flaky-digests}}
 {:seon.task/name "an-agents-prove-defers-a-process-test-it-cannot-run"
  :seon.task/plan "Dogfood 0.7 (an outside agent, 2026-10-27), found proving my.task/status-answers-before-any-turn through seon_eval and seon_prove. A deftest written at the agent's REPL that calls seon.agent-test/in-agent errors in (my.agent/prove) with \"no process store at .../target/runs/test-store-..: bin/test makes it (its plan: make-process-store!) before any shard starts; run the test through bin/test\". (my.agent/tests-reaching 'seon.test-store/shared-root!) lists the new test as reaching in-agent, which is in :seon.config.source/process-vars, yet (:deferred (my.agent/prove)) stayed [] and :at-judge 16: the test ran in the agent's fork instead of being deferred to the merge gate as my.agent/deferred says it is. Find why a test defined at the REPL is not classified a process test (the branch's reach graph not refreshed for a new test? deferral not applied to a registered proof?) and fix it at that producer. Proof: a process test defined on a branch is :deferred by (my.agent/prove), not run and not an error."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/a-process-test-defined-on-a-branch-is-deferred-not-run}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-that-calls-the-turn-path-does-not-recurse-into-prove"
  :seon.task/plan "Dogfood 0.7, form: in an agent, a deftest that clears (swap! seon.agent/state update :turn dissoc :observed) and calls (my.task/status), where status measures through seon.turn/observe, makes (my.agent/prove) answer :error \"test process died: {:exit 0} while #'seon.task-test/status-answers-before-any-turn ran\". Suspected cause (not confirmed): observe asks the test service (seon.agent/prove!) from inside the test service's own fork, which waits on its own run or exits. Confirm with the fork's stderr, then make a test that reaches the turn path either run its observe without asking the service for the run it is already in, or answer the typed error, never a dead child. Proof: a test that calls seon.turn/observe inside a prove run ends with its own result."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/a-test-in-a-prove-run-that-reaches-observe-ends-with-its-result}}
 {:seon.task/name "test-results-are-a-few-facts"
  :seon.task/plan "Not ready: the owner (Sean) must say whether bin/test and bin/prove write facts through root at all: decisions.md (2026-10-28, \"Agent runs only\") and docs/merging.md#verdicts-are-a-cache keep the file cache as what reuse reads and bin/land/full-run rely on a file surviving a store erase, which contradicts the plan's \"delete the file cache\"; the built part (agent run facts) stands. Test results go back into the database as a few facts per test and per function, through root's one writer, with no
history, replacing slice 1's file cache (8564dea1d5, ``target/results``). Owner rulings: \"one writer for sure\"; \"never
300k new records per test run … just the essentials\" (2026-10-27); \"We want to be able to query things in the db,
functions, tests, schemas and understand what tests are failing and timings\" (2026-10-28); derived data may be
cached, always regenerable (docs/data-modeling.md#derived-data-is-a-cache-never-relied-on).

Built (step R2a, scoped 2026-10-29 to agent runs): a test's var entity carries `:seon.test/digest`, `:seon.test/status` (:green :red :flaky :deferred), `:seon.test/failures`, `:seon.test/ticks` and `:seon.test/bytes`, all :seon.db/no-history? (resources/seon/schemas/seon.facts.edn), written on the agent's branch by `seon.agent/record-results!` through `seon.proofs/result-tx` when a run ends (a run at the digest adds to the sums, a new digest resets them); a function's measurements are `:seon.fn/stats` (calls, ticks, self-ticks, bytes, hist, worst). The agent's status (`seon.ctx.status/status`) reads them there.

Not built: bin/test and bin/prove still write and read the shared file cache (`seon.fx/write-verdicts!`, `seon.fx/read-verdict`, `seon.fx/stored-results`, and the private key-dir, verdict-path, verdict-files; the config key `:seon.config.proofs/results-dir`; bin/env's SEON_RESULTS_DIR), and so do `seon.agent/stored-results` and the judge's reuse. The rest of this task moves them: bin/test / bin/prove shards write through root's one writer on main (root not running: the run says UNRECORDED, an error as data, never silently), a stored result is a fact read where it was read from the file, and the file cache and its keys are deleted (their ~150 lines, the config key, SEON_RESULTS_DIR, the per-check target/tmp/check-results.* dirs, `seon.test-store`'s results-dir). A shared store keeps cross-checkout reuse, so this step has its own design first.

Proofs, to write red first: a test run writes one fact set per test that ran and nothing for a reused one (count datoms per run); two runs at one digest give runs 2 and the summed ms; a run at a new digest resets them; red to green retracts the failure; green and red at one digest is :flaky, rerun after the code changes; noHistory holds; an unchanged tree's second `bin/prove` runs nothing (reads the facts); root down: `bin/test` answers UNRECORDED and exits non-zero; the eval answer stays under its bound with 300 reds; cost proof: writing a run's results costs O(tests run), not O(all tests). Done when all are green, the file cache and its keys are gone, docs/merging.md#verdicts-are-a-cache and docs/storage.md say so, `bin/prove` is green, and a one-function prove's result lookup is still milliseconds."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-flaky-test-is-a-task"
  :seon.task/plan "The owner, 2026-10-30: \"we shouldn't write tests that drift like that -- so a flaky test is a mining and task to fix it dispatched to an agent\". What exists: seon.proofs/flaky marks a test the runner saw green and red at one digest (:flaky true, judged red until its digest moves), and bin/prove prints \"N flaky\"; nothing more happens, so a flaky test stays red and unowned (2026-10-30: three load-sensitive agent tests held flaky by run 29's gate). Correct: the first time a test is judged flaky at a digest, root writes a task for it from a template, as seon.template/stall writes a stall's (one task per test and digest, its name; its cause pre-filled: the test, both results with their failures, the run logs' paths, the load the runs saw), released for a Seon agent to find the cause and fix it at its cause (a clock, a race, live state: never a retry or a wider bound). It is the first kind of one mechanism, a finding becomes a task (docs/health.md#a-form-too-long: a template per kind), so the shrink kinds reuse it. Deletes nothing; replaces bin/prove's print-only flaky count as the place a flaky test goes."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/a-test-green-and-red-at-one-digest-becomes-one-task}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-prove-ended-ends-its-shards"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-02 (the optimization session): `timeout 1500 bin/prove --merge` ended at its bound under load 15-19, and its test-runner shards (target/jolt/jolt -A:test run -m test-runner --shard ..., ppid 1, their own process group) went on running for 20 minutes, starting test roots, until killed by hand. bin/prove's children do not end with it. The mechanism exists for ./seon processes (task seon.lib/a-child-ends-with-its-starter: a C thread ends a process when what it watches exits); bin/test's shards are not under it. Correct: a shard runner watches the prove (or bin/test) that started it and ends, with its process group, when it is gone, so a killed prove leaves nothing running."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.check-test/a-shard-ends-with-the-prove-that-started-it}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-test-parked-on-a-descriptor-holds-every-agent"
  :seon.task/plan "What exists: `seon.prove` runs one test run at a time per checkout, and an agent's test child ends by its tick budget, its death, or Jolt's all-asleep check (docs/merging.md#a-test-child-is-a-fork-of-a-live-agent). A test parked on a descriptor nothing will ever write, a pipe it holds both ends of, is none of these: a descriptor awaited can wake it, so it is never told, and every other agent's turn waits behind it on the one slot. Seen as the promise case in deepseek-run3, whose cause is fixed; this one is left. What correct looks like: one agent's stuck run never stops another agent's turn, by running each branch's run on a slot of its own under a bound of work (a config key, no clock), its wait recorded as what the stuck agent waits on. Replaces the one `:running` of `seon.prove`."
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.prove-test/a-stuck-run-of-one-branch-leaves-another-branchs-run-going}}
 {:seon.task/name "a-tests-ticks-are-the-same-alone-and-in-a-run"
  :seon.task/plan "Seen 2026-11-10 (Context's baseline, see seon.agent/a-done-counts-only-the-reds-its-change-reaches): seon.program-test/a-test-that-starts-a-process-reaches-the-program-it-runs and seon.render-test/a-render-reaches-no-process went over their 4B-tick budget (:seon.config.agent/test-ticks) inside a big prove and were green alone. Ticks are work done and should be the same for one test on any machine, at any load, in any company; a count that differs by what ran before is a defect of the count or of the test. The class: a budget that a process's history can turn red (AGENTS.md section 5: no assertion the machine's load can turn red; history is the same class). Candidates, to reproduce in this order (inferred, not yet run): (a) collections: garbage earlier tests left is collected inside this test's span, and the budget in seon.proofs/run-tests! counts it (seon.cost/measure takes off a tick allowance a collection; the budget may not); (b) derived data a process holds (a kept reach graph, my.test's world delay, a program db) warm in one process and built in the other, both tests walking the whole program's reach; (c) Jolt's one-way switch to threaded lazy seqs after a process's first OS thread (seon.cost's `threaded`). Reproduce: run each test alone and after the tests its shard ran before it in Context's run (the shard file names them), compare ticks and the collections in its span. Fix at the cause: a count with no collection work, or a test that makes the data it reads. Proof, red first: one of the two tests run after a test that leaves a large heap, its ticks within a fixed slack of its ticks alone."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/seon-program-test-test-starts-process-reaches-program}}
{:seon.task/name "a-check-steps-digest-takes-the-seed-of-the-store-it-runs-on"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-09 in jolt/erase-window. jolt/cite-ref at 958ffb11cd moved its standing targets (the sections and var every agent and template cites) out of seon.db/seed-tx, and its bin/prove reused the green verdicts of e2e-conflict, e2e-semantic, e2e-hairy, e2e-newns, e2e-sibling, e2e-held and swarm. Their digests (seon.proofs/step-digests: the reach of each step's functions) did not move, because a step reads the check store, which another step (seed) made from seon.db/seed-tx and seon.test-store/standing-targets-tx, and neither is in the step's reach. In the erase window the same steps were red: each merge task's create was refused, :seon.db/no-target [:var/name my.branch/merge-main!], since the check store lacked the var the merge template's instructions cite. Reproduction: on a tree whose seed lacks the template's cited var, a check step that writes a merge task keeps its old green digest and reuses it, while bin/check --steps runs it red. The check store is keyed by its seed's digest since 2026-11-09 (seon.check/seed: the link target-dir check-store-<digest of [schema-digest, seed-tx, standing-targets-tx]>). Correct: each check step that runs on the check store takes that seed digest into its digest (step-digests' input beside :env), so a changed seed moves every such step's digest and runs it. Proof, red first: a change to standing-targets-tx moves an e2e step's digest."
  :seon.task/kind :work
  :seon.task/proofs #{seon.proofs-test/change-seon-test-store-standing-targets-tx-seed}}
 {:seon.task/name "tests-leave-no-scratch-behind"
  :seon.task/plan "Found 2026-11-10 by the slow-test helpers: tests leave scratch directories they make (target/tmp/prove-* from seon.proofs-test's runs, 25 in one worktree; target/stalls-test-*; a temp dir of seon.fx-test's source-read test). Correct: each removes what it makes in a finally, and the full run's end names any target/tmp entry younger than it (a leak is red, as a thread left running is). Proof: the full run's leak check red on a test that leaves one."
  :seon.task/kind :work
